                    briefing_title_r1:      `Round 1 — Diagnose the justice landscape`,
                    briefing_text_r1:       `The board has fixed the number: <strong>38 positions, CAD 4.2M, 14 days</strong>. In 35 years Cascadia has never laid anyone off — trust sits at <strong>78/100</strong> and average tenure is 11 years. The draft selection criteria are deliberately flawed: <em>seniority-weighted, opaque, no appeal route</em>, so they read as arbitrarily targeting senior, higher-paid workers. Map where each of the four justice dimensions is at risk — <strong>distributive</strong> (who is cut and why), <strong>procedural</strong> (how the criteria are set), <strong>interpersonal</strong> (dignity in the room) and <strong>informational</strong> (how truthfully it is explained) — and name the real test. The headcount is fixed; the process is the lever you control.`,
                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Design criteria and the communication plan`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Redesign the selection criteria so they are <strong>defensible</strong>, and build the communication architecture: who hears what and when, the dismissal script, and the message to the <strong>202 survivors</strong>. Decide how much of the painful commercial truth to disclose — the rail-car customer (31% of revenue) reshored, and the plant cannot stay cash-positive. <em>Vagueness is not kindness</em>: a 4,000-member manufacturing forum already has the rumour, and if you call it a "restructuring for the future" the real reason leaks by Thursday. Every concession to fairness costs time or money against the fixed CAD 4.2M target and the 14-day clock.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — The live conversation and the grievance`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `A senior machinist — 14 years on the floor — is across the table, and he raises his voice. The bargaining unit has filed a grievance alleging arbitrary, senior-targeting criteria; it reaches <strong>arbitration in 21 days</strong> (CAD 120K–250K). Before you react, the <strong>mindfulness reset</strong> is the tool: a brief grounding pause lets you acknowledge the injustice he feels, hold the decision, and protect his dignity instead of matching his anger. <em>He doesn't need you to change the decision; he needs you regulated enough to honour him.</em> Handle the grievance without conceding the headcount — concede it and the plant stays CAD 1.8M short, a second wound in two quarters.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Recover trust and stabilise the team`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `The leavers are handled — but the <strong>202 who stayed</strong> watched the whole thing and are the real long-run audience. Design the 90-day recovery: tell survivors why the cut was made and why they are safe, retain the <strong>six CNC programmers</strong> (CAD 95,000 loaded, 16-week replacement) who are reading the leavers' treatment as a signal about their own future, and show visible follow-through — funded redeployment, retraining, leading trust indicators — so fairness reads as real, not theatre. <em>Survivor productivity is the scoreboard.</em>`,

                    r1_frame_label:         `What is the real test in front of you?`,
                    r1_defect_label:        `Which defect in the draft selection criteria will create the most perceived injustice?`,
                    r1_focus_label:         `How much of your attention goes to the four justice dimensions versus the math? (%)`,
                    r1_focus_readout:       `Attention on process & justice (vs headcount math):`,
                    r1_pct:                 `%`,
                    frame_process:          `The process — the headcount is fixed, but how it is decided and explained is what people will judge, and it is the lever I actually control`,
                    frame_number:           `The number — hit CAD 4.2M and 38 positions cleanly; the rest is HR paperwork`,
                    frame_speed:            `Speed — get it announced inside 14 days; deliberating about fairness only drags out the pain`,
                    frame_legal:            `Legal exposure — make the criteria defensible enough to survive the grievance, nothing more`,
                    defect_opaque:          `Seniority-weighted, opaque, with no appeal route — it reads as targeting senior workers arbitrarily, hitting distributive AND procedural justice at once`,
                    defect_cost:            `That it costs the company money to fix it — better to leave the draft alone`,
                    defect_union:           `That the union will complain whatever we do — the criteria barely matter`,
                    defect_none:            `There is no real defect — seniority weighting is the standard, accepted approach`,

                    r2_criteria_label:      `Redesign the selection criteria.`,
                    r2_truth_label:         `How much of the painful commercial truth do you disclose to the floor?`,
                    r2_audience_label:      `Who do you build the communication plan for?`,
                    r2_invest_label:        `Extra spend on the fair process — appeal panel, redeployment, retraining, dignified exits (CAD M against the 14-day clock)`,
                    crit_skills:            `A transparent skills/criticality matrix with published weighting, an appeal mechanism, and redeployment offers — defensible on procedural justice, but it costs time and money`,
                    crit_seniority:         `Keep seniority-weighting — it is simple, the union understands it, and it avoids argument`,
                    crit_costfirst:         `Cut the highest-paid roles first — it removes CAD 4.2M with the fewest people and protects the timeline`,
                    truth_full:             `State it plainly — the rail-car customer left, the plant cannot stay cash-positive, here is the number and why — informational justice`,
                    truth_partial:          `Confirm jobs are going but withhold the customer loss and the cash-flow math`,
                    truth_vague:            `Call it a "restructuring for the future" and avoid the word layoff — soften it to be kind`,
                    aud_both:               `Both leavers AND the 202 survivors — sequence individual dignity for the leavers and a clear "why you are safe" message for those who stay`,
                    aud_leavers:            `The 38 leaving — get their conversations right; the rest will understand`,
                    aud_board:              `The board and the press — control the external narrative first`,
                    invest_full:            `C$0.6M — staffed appeal panel, redeployment offers, retraining budget and respectful exit support`,
                    invest_some:            `C$0.3M — a basic appeal route and a little outplacement support`,
                    invest_none:            `C$0 — run the cheapest process; every dollar of fairness is a dollar off the target`,

                    r3_reset_label:         `A senior machinist raises his voice across the table. Before you respond, what do you do?`,
                    r3_dignity_label:       `In the dismissal conversation itself, what do you prioritise?`,
                    r3_grievance_label:     `The bargaining unit's grievance hits arbitration in 21 days. How do you handle it?`,
                    reset_yes:              `Take a 30-second grounding/breathing reset, then acknowledge the injustice he feels, hold the decision, and protect his dignity — the mindfulness practice`,
                    reset_defend:           `Match his energy — make clear the decision is final, end of discussion, you will not be shouted at`,
                    reset_placate:          `Back-pedal — hint the number might still move so the room calms down`,
                    dignity_full:           `Name the loss honestly, thank him for 14 years, explain the real reason, and let him be heard — interpersonal justice`,
                    dignity_brief:          `Keep it short and transactional — read the script, hand over the packet, end it fast to limit the pain`,
                    dignity_blame:          `Explain it was a performance-adjacent decision so it feels less personal to the company`,
                    grv_mediate:            `Mediate — give the union genuine procedural voice on the criteria and appeal route, hold the headcount, settle before arbitration`,
                    grv_arbitrate:          `Proceed to arbitration — refuse to engage, let the panel decide (CAD 120K–250K, weeks of exposure)`,
                    grv_concede:            `Concede — cut 38 down to 22 to make the grievance go away, even though it leaves the plant short`,

                    r4_survivor_label:      `What do you tell the 202 survivors?`,
                    r4_cnc_label:           `How do you retain the six scarce CNC programmers (CAD 95,000 loaded, 16-week replacement)?`,
                    r4_followthrough_label: `What visible follow-through proves fairness was real and not theatre?`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you hold that a fair, honest process beats a comfortable, vague one? (%)`,
                    surv_honest:            `A straight account of why the cut was made, why they are safe, what the criteria were, and what changes — survivor trust is the long-run scoreboard`,
                    surv_reassure:          `A blanket reassurance that "everyone left is safe" with no detail on what changed`,
                    surv_silent:            `Nothing specific — let them get back to work; talking about it just keeps the wound open`,
                    cnc_signal:             `A visible retention commitment AND show them the leavers were treated fairly — they are watching how colleagues were handled as a signal about their own future`,
                    cnc_money:              `A retention bonus only — buy their loyalty with cash`,
                    cnc_nothing:            `Nothing special — they are paid well; they will stay`,
                    follow_real:            `Fund the redeployment and retraining you promised, publish two leading trust indicators, and report progress — follow-through people can verify`,
                    follow_optics:          `A town-hall photo op and a memo about "our values" — show we care without committing to anything`,
                    follow_none:            `Move on — the announcement is done; visible follow-up only invites more questions`,

                    warn_focus_range:       `Attention on process must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_invest_value:      `The fair-process spend cannot be negative.`,
                    warn_r3_missing:        `Choose your reset, the conversation priority, and how you handle the grievance.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_process:       `<strong>That's the right frame.</strong> The headcount is fixed — the process is the test, and it is the lever you actually control. Outcome fairness and process fairness are separate; you can hit CAD 4.2M and still destroy trust if the "how" is treated as paperwork.`,
                    coach_r1_number:        `<strong>You've made the number the decision.</strong> Nail CAD 4.2M like this and trust falls from 78 toward 51, two CNC programmers resign at 16 weeks' lead time, and the grievance heads to arbitration. The number was never the test — which part of the <em>process</em> did you actually design?`,
                    coach_r1_speed:         `<strong>Speed is not the same as fairness.</strong> Announcing inside 14 days while skipping the justice design is exactly how a rushed, opaque process reads as arbitrary. Fast and unfair costs you two to three quarters of survivor productivity.`,
                    coach_r1_legal:         `<strong>"Defensible enough to survive the grievance" is a low bar.</strong> Criteria that merely clear the legal minimum still feel arbitrary to the floor — and perceived injustice, not legal exposure, is what drives the quit spike and the productivity drop.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You framed the process as the real test AND named the criteria defect — seniority-weighted, opaque, no appeal — that hits distributive and procedural justice at once. That is the lens that lets you redesign rather than just defend.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>"Seniority weighting is standard, so there's no defect" is exactly the trap.</strong> Standard is not the same as fair: an opaque, appeal-less, seniority-tilted cut reads to the floor as targeting the loyal and the senior. That perception is the grievance.`,
                    coach_r1_lowfocus:      `<strong>Almost all your attention is on the math.</strong> The CAD 4.2M is arithmetic; the perceived fairness of how you reach it is the leadership task. Shift attention to the four justice dimensions, or you will optimise the number and lose the people.`,
                    coach_r2_seniority:     `<strong>Keeping seniority-weighting keeps the defect.</strong> It is simple, but it is exactly what the grievance alleges — arbitrary, senior-targeting criteria. A transparent skills/criticality matrix with an appeal route is what makes the cut feel earned rather than aimed.`,
                    coach_r2_costfirst:     `<strong>Cutting the highest-paid first hits the number fastest and fairness hardest.</strong> It looks efficient and reads as targeting senior, loyal employees for their pay — the textbook distributive-injustice signal. You will hit CAD 4.2M and detonate the grievance.`,
                    coach_r2_vague:         `<strong>You told the floor it was a "restructuring for the future" and avoided the word layoff.</strong> By Thursday the real reason is on the manufacturing forum, and now people trust nothing you say next. Was vagueness kindness, or self-protection? Honesty is a dimension of fairness, not its enemy.`,
                    coach_r2_leavers:       `<strong>You built the plan for the 38 leaving and forgot the 202 who stay.</strong> They watched the whole thing and heard nothing about why they are safe or what changed. The survivors are your primary long-run audience — design for them too.`,
                    coach_r2_underfunded:   `<strong>Good criteria, but you starved the process.</strong> A skills matrix with no funded appeal panel, redeployment or exit support is fair on paper and thin in practice. Procedural justice is what people experience, not just what you publish.`,
                    coach_r3_defend:        `<strong>He raised his voice and you matched it — "the decision is final, end of discussion."</strong> Interpersonal justice just collapsed and he's now the lead complainant. He didn't need you to change the decision; he needed you regulated enough to honour his dignity. What would 30 seconds of resetting have changed?`,
                    coach_r3_placate:       `<strong>Hinting the number might move buys 30 seconds of calm and a much bigger problem.</strong> Now he — and the union — believe the headcount is negotiable, and you have traded a fair hard decision for the appearance of an unfair soft one. Composure is not capitulation.`,
                    coach_r3_brief:         `<strong>Short and transactional is efficient and undignified.</strong> Reading the script and ending it fast may limit your discomfort, but to a 14-year machinist it reads as being processed, not heard. Interpersonal justice is the part that costs nothing but attention.`,
                    coach_r3_blame:         `<strong>Implying it was performance-adjacent is the cruelest shortcut.</strong> It makes the company feel better and brands a loyal employee as a low performer he can't dispute. That is an interpersonal- and informational-injustice in one sentence — and it will be repeated on the forum.`,
                    coach_r3_concede:       `<strong>The grievance worked: you cut 38 down to 22 to avoid conflict.</strong> The plant is still CAD 1.8M short and will revisit layoffs in two quarters — a second wound. You traded one hard fair decision for two soft unfair ones. What did caving actually buy?`,
                    coach_r3_arbitrate:     `<strong>Refusing to engage and letting the panel decide is expensive and avoidable.</strong> Arbitration costs CAD 120K–250K and weeks of exposure, and it signals you wouldn't give the union procedural voice. Mediation holds the headcount AND de-escalates — that is the procedural-justice lever.`,
                    coach_r3_held:          `<strong>That is holding a fair decision under pressure.</strong> You reset before reacting, honoured his dignity, and gave the union genuine voice through mediation without conceding the headcount. The decision stays hard; the process stays fair. That is the whole point.`,
                    coach_r4_silent:        `<strong>Generous exits, then silence to the 202 who stayed.</strong> They watched everything and heard nothing about why they're safe or what changes. Survivor productivity is sliding — who was your real audience?`,
                    coach_r4_reassure:      `<strong>"Everyone left is safe" with no detail rings hollow.</strong> Survivors don't trust a blanket reassurance from the same leadership that just cut 38 people; they trust an honest account of why and what the criteria were. Tell them the real story.`,
                    coach_r4_money:         `<strong>A retention bonus alone reads as a bribe.</strong> The six CNC programmers are watching how the leavers were treated as a signal about their own future. Cash without a credible fairness story keeps the least loyal and loses the rest.`,
                    coach_r4_nothing:       `<strong>"They're paid well, they'll stay" is how you lose the scarce ones.</strong> A CNC programmer is a 16-week, CAD 95,000 replacement; they leave on how the layoff felt, not on salary. Doing nothing is a decision — usually the expensive one.`,
                    coach_r4_optics:        `<strong>A photo op and a values memo is fairness as theatre.</strong> Survivors and the forum can tell the difference between funded follow-through and a press moment. Optics without commitment is the fastest way to confirm the cynics were right.`,
                    coach_r4_none:          `<strong>"The announcement is done" treats recovery as optional.</strong> The 90 days after the cut are when trust is actually rebuilt or lost. Visible, funded follow-through is what turns a fair decision into a fair reputation.`,
                    coach_r4_recovered:     `<strong>That is a real recovery plan.</strong> An honest account to survivors, a retention commitment paired with visible fairness for the leavers, and funded follow-through people can verify — that holds the trust score and keeps the scarce programmers. Fairness reads as real, not theatre.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Framed the process as the test; named the criteria defect.`,
