                    sum_r3_caved:           `Conceded the headcount — second wound coming.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Conversation or grievance handled under fairness.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Honest to survivors, funded follow-through, programmers kept.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Neglected the survivors or the follow-through.`,

                    insight_just:           `Just & trusted: you treated the process as the real decision — defensible criteria, an honest commercial explanation, a self-regulated conversation that held the line, and a recovery plan the survivors could verify. Trust held above the danger zone, the grievance settled without conceding the headcount, and the scarce programmers stayed. The number was always the easy part; you passed the actual test.`,
                    insight_caved:          `Caved: when the union pushed, you cut 38 down to 22 to avoid the conflict. The plant is still CAD 1.8M short and will revisit layoffs within two quarters — a second wound on a workforce that now knows the headcount is negotiable. You traded one hard fair decision for repeated soft unfair ones.`,
                    insight_trustfell:      `Trust collapsed: the process read as unfair, and trust fell through the ~55 danger zone that historically precedes voluntary-quit spikes among your retained high performers. The cut hit CAD 4.2M on paper while the survivor productivity loss and replacement costs quietly cost you more.`,
                    insight_short:          `You didn't fully reconcile outcome and process. The cut landed, but enough fairness was sacrificed — in the criteria, the conversation, or the survivors' experience — that the trust recovery is partial and the grievance left a mark.`,
                    insight_leaked:         `You softened the message to "be kind", and the real reason leaked to the manufacturing forum. Informational justice broke, and now people discount whatever you say next — vagueness cost you more trust than the truth would have.`,
                    insight_reactive:       `Your composure ran out in the live conversation. You reacted to anger you knew was partly justified instead of resetting first, and the interpersonal-justice damage outlasted the exchange. Mindfulness here was a fairness tool, not a perk.`,
                    insight_survivors:      `You optimised for the people leaving and under-served the 202 who stayed. They were the primary long-run stakeholder, and their unanswered "why am I safe, and was that fair?" is where the survivor-productivity loss came from.`,
                    insight_cnc:            `You lost scarce CNC programmers — each a 16-week, CAD 95,000 replacement. They left on how the layoff felt, not on pay; the fairness of the process was their retention lever, and it wasn't strong enough.`,
                    insight_cnckept:        `You kept all six CNC programmers. They read the fair treatment of the leavers and the honest survivor message as a signal about their own future — fairness, not a bonus, was what held the hardest-to-replace talent.`,

                    hint_caved:             `You conceded the headcount — the plant is CAD 1.8M short and a second layoff is coming. Holding a fair hard decision beats avoiding the conflict; rebuild credibility by being unambiguous from here.`,
                    hint_leaked:            `The real reason leaked because the message was vague. Recover informational justice fast: be plainly honest with survivors about why the cut was made and why they are safe.`,
                    hint_composure:         `Your composure is low — you are reacting rather than leading. Reset before each charged exchange; honouring anger without matching it is what protects interpersonal justice.`,
                    hint_trust:             `Trust is below the ~55 danger zone — retained high performers start leaving here. Lead with an honest survivor message and visible, funded follow-through to pull it back.`,
                    hint_survivors:         `You are designing for the leavers and ignoring the 202 who stay. They are the long-run audience; tell them why they are safe and what changed, or survivor productivity keeps sliding.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — trust, fairness and survivor productivity all healthy. Lock it in with an honest survivor message and funded follow-through so the fairness reads as real.`,

